# Bellows-Polarity Bouyon

**Ingredients:** ANGLO CONCERTINA + BOUYON + PREPARED ELECTRIC PIANO + TANBOU / WOODBLOCK + BASS FLUTE + SHORT SYNTH BASS

**Creative Catalyst:** The genre began during a hurricane-season residency in a decommissioned cable-car inspection shed above Roseau, Dominica, where Anglo-concertina player Maeve Baptiste was rehearsing dance figures while producer Kervin Joseph programmed compact bouyon drum loops beside a rack of retired traction relays. The breakthrough came when Kervin focused on the Anglo concertina’s bisonoric logic: the same button produces one pitch while the bellows are pushed and another when they are pulled. Instead of treating bellows direction as an accompaniment detail, he made PUSH and PULL into compositional states. Kick, percussion, bass, and electric-piano voicings were assigned different behaviors depending on which direction the bellows were moving. Prepared electric piano, tanbou, woodblock, bass flute, and short synth bass completed a style where harmonic motion can happen simply because the player reverses air pressure without changing finger position.

Bellows-Polarity Bouyon is a fast, compact acoustic-electronic dance form built from directional harmony. Bouyon supplies the chassis: 148–160 BPM, short hard kick, rapid snare and clap punctuation, repeating percussion cells, clipped bass phrases, bright rhythmic keyboards, shouted vocal fragments, and dense but economical loops designed for continuous forward motion. The Anglo concertina supplies the central contradiction. One physical button is not one stable note. Its pitch depends on whether the bellows are compressing or expanding.

The concertina is recorded with a close stereo pair aimed toward the two end boxes, one small ribbon microphone positioned above the bellows fold, and one restrained room microphone. The stereo pair captures reed attack, button noise, side-to-side image, and the tonal difference between the two ends. The ribbon emphasizes bellows breath, leather movement, frame creak, and the pressure change that occurs at reversal. The room microphone rises only during fully expanded PULL passages so the physical size of the bellows becomes audible without turning the instrument into an ambient pad.

Players favor repeated single-button figures, two-button dyads, tonic-fifth toggles, suspended seconds, modal thirds, short pentatonic cells, octave punctuation, cross-row fingering, and intentionally repeated finger shapes whose pitches alternate because the bellows reverse. Long lyrical folk melody is minimized. The defining gesture is a finger that stays in place while the harmony changes underneath it because PUSH becomes PULL.

The central compositional device is **Polarity Lock**. A phrase is built around one fingering shape and two pressure states: PUSH and PULL. During PUSH, the rhythm section is assigned one harmonic and percussive behavior; during PULL, it is assigned another. The performer may keep the same buttons depressed across the reversal, causing the acoustic pitch set to flip immediately while the physical hand position remains unchanged.

A second device, **Direction Debt**, delays the electronic response to a bellows reversal. When the concertina changes from PUSH to PULL, the drums may continue the previous-state pattern for one beat or half a bar before updating. This temporary mismatch creates propulsion without syncopating the master tempo.

A third device, **Fold Snap**, uses the exact moment of bellows reversal as a structural transient. The player briefly reduces pressure, changes direction, and re-engages the reeds. A woodblock, tanbou rim, clipped clap, or short bass pulse can land immediately after the reversal, turning a mechanical change in airflow into the equivalent of a drum pickup.

A fourth device, **Button Mirage**, repeats the same visible fingering pattern over several bars while the sequence of PUSH and PULL lengths changes. The listener hears new melodic cells even though the player’s finger map barely changes. This allows complex phrase variation without turning the concertina into a conventional solo instrument.

Bouyon drums stay fast, dry, and physically direct. Kick is compact and frequent but not sub-heavy. Snare and clap are short. Percussion uses rapid repeated cells, syncopated rim patterns, bright metal ticks, and small hand-drum accents. High-frequency density is reduced during exposed Fold Snaps so concertina button and reed attacks remain legible.

The short synth bass uses root, fifth, octave, flat seventh, and brief chromatic approaches. It changes behavior according to Polarity Lock. PUSH may receive short tonic/octave punctuation; PULL may receive fifth/seventh answers or silence. Long 808 glides, sustained sub drones, and bass lines that continuously shadow the concertina are excluded.

Prepared electric piano supplies clipped harmonic checkpoints. Selected tines are muted with removable felt or paper. Voicings favor suspended seconds, sixths, open fifths, minor sevenths, and bright two-note upper shells. The piano is often silent during the first beat after a bellows reversal, allowing Direction Debt to remain audible before harmony catches up.

Tanbou provides low-mid acoustic punctuation through palm tones, dry center hits, edge slaps, finger doubles, and short rolls. Woodblock provides narrow transients around Fold Snap. These acoustic percussion sources replace selected machine hits instead of doubling the whole groove.

Bass flute supplies breath-heavy lower-mid motion through soft fundamentals, tongue stops, octave drops, air-only attacks, key noise, and short falling thirds. It is most active during long PULL passages, when the expanding bellows create broader harmonic space and the synth bass becomes simpler.

Vocals are optional and sparse. Dominican Creole, English, or French fragments may reference cable cars, inspection numbers, folds, push-pull instructions, uphill and downhill runs, pressure marks, or phrases such as “push two,” “pull four,” “same button,” and “change direction.” Delivery is shouted, spoken, or tightly chanted rather than built into a large sung chorus.

Effects remain restrained. Concertina stays mostly dry with a short wooden-room reflection and compact plate only on long PULL sustains. Electric piano gets a tiny spring or plate. Bass flute gets a dark chamber. Drum bus compression is firm and fast. No Celtic-session reverb, cinematic accordion pads, tropical EDM risers, long sub tails, or sampled concertina loops are allowed.

The defining tension is fixed finger position versus changing airflow. The hand can remain still while the bellows reverse the note, and the entire bouyon arrangement reacts to that hidden mechanical polarity.

## Genre DNA

**Tempo & Pulse:** 148–160 BPM, 4/4 bouyon architecture with compact kick, rapid snare/clap punctuation, repeating percussion cells, short bass replies, and concertina phrases whose harmonic identity changes with bellows direction.

**Harmonic Palette:** Dorian, Mixolydian, Aeolian, open fifths, suspended seconds, sixths, minor sevenths, pentatonic cells, and two-state harmonic relationships created by bisonoric PUSH/PULL fingering.

**Production Signatures:**

1. **Polarity Lock** — one concertina fingering shape generates different acoustic pitch sets in PUSH and PULL states.
2. **Direction Debt** — the electronic arrangement intentionally reacts late to a bellows reversal for one subdivision or short phrase.
3. **Fold Snap** — the pressure-reset transient at bellows reversal becomes a cue for percussion or bass punctuation.
4. **Button Mirage** — stable finger patterns produce changing phrases because PUSH/PULL durations are rearranged.
5. **Reversal Clearance** — dense hats and upper percussion thin around exposed direction changes.
6. **No-Sampled-Concertina Rule** — principal concertina material remains live and bisonoric; no one-shot loop may replace the PUSH/PULL logic.

**Dynamics:** Intensity grows through shorter PUSH/PULL alternation, harder bellows pressure, denser bouyon percussion, brighter electric-piano stabs, stronger tanbou accents, and increasingly delayed Direction Debt responses. Climaxes remain tight and fast rather than huge.

# Scene & Origin Lore

Bellows-Polarity Bouyon formed inside a retired cable-car inspection and traction-control shed in the hills above Roseau, Dominica. The site retained steel sheaves, traction relays, grease-marked cable drums, numbered brake components, faded inspection boards, red hand signals, corrugated service panels, sloped concrete floors, storm shutters, and a narrow window looking down toward the coast.

Anglo-concertina player **Maeve Baptiste** had been using the shed because the dry wooden control room made reed response and bellows noise easy to hear even during heavy tropical rain. She became fascinated by the fact that one depressed button could produce two different notes depending on whether the instrument was being compressed or expanded.

Bouyon producer **Kervin Joseph** arrived with a compact drum sampler, short-bass synth, and a set of fast stripped rhythms. His first arrangement followed Maeve’s audible note changes like a conventional melody. It sounded busy and predictable. He then ignored the actual note names and wrote two different drum states labeled PUSH and PULL. The result immediately made bellows direction feel like a hidden conductor.

Prepared-electric-piano player **Solène Laurent** joined after designing short harmonic responses that could deliberately arrive late after reversals. Percussionist **Jamal Etienne** reduced his setup to one tanbou, one woodblock, and one small metal plate. Bass-flutist **Naomi Clarke** completed the ensemble with low breath and air-only attacks for synth-bass-light sections.

Their first performances were called **direction tests**. Two industrial lamps behind the band were labeled PUSH and PULL. They were changed manually by Jamal during rehearsed structural points and did not control any electronics. The lights existed only as a visual listening guide.

The visual language developed around traction red, storm-cloud grey, oxidized steel, inspection-card cream, concertina mahogany, cable-grease black, weathered yellow paint, and tropical green visible through open shutters. The unofficial rule is that if a listener can understand the entire harmonic change without hearing the bellows reverse, the arrangement is too conventional.

