# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Push Two, Pull Four at Tower Nine

**Story & Context:** At 156 BPM, Maeve holds one two-button fingering shape for the opening section. Two bars of PUSH create a compact tonic-fifth cell while Kervin runs a kick-heavy bouyon pattern. Four bars of PULL flip the concertina pitches without changing the hand shape; the drums continue the PUSH pattern for one extra beat, creating the first Direction Debt before moving into a brighter snare-and-rim variation. Solène waits until the debt resolves, then strikes one prepared-Wurlitzer sixth. Jamal places a woodblock immediately after every second Fold Snap. Naomi enters only during the longest PULL cycle with one low breathy bass-flute fundamental. The track’s hook is audible harmonic motion created by the bellows while the player’s fingers barely move.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Cable-car inspection diagram marked TOWER 9, two arrows labeled PUSH 2 and PULL 4, one concertina button map remaining visually unchanged while colored note labels flip beside it, storm clouds opening over green hills. Sparse vocal fragments limited to “push two,” “pull four,” “tower nine,” and “same button.”

## Track 2: Brake Relay Owes One Beat

**Story & Context:** At 160 BPM, Kervin builds the EP’s most aggressive percussion loop around Direction Debt. Every time Maeve reverses bellows direction, kick and bass stay in the previous polarity for exactly one beat before switching. The delayed electronic response creates a repeating lean-forward sensation without changing tempo. Solène uses clipped suspended-second voicings only after the debt is paid. Jamal alternates tanbou edge slaps with dry woodblock responses. Midway through, Maeve performs a Button Mirage by repeating the exact same fingering while shortening the PUSH cycle from four beats to two, then one, causing increasingly rapid pitch alternation. Naomi remains silent until the final breakdown, where only concertina, tanbou, and breathy flute survive.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Old traction relay labeled BRAKE with one timing line shifted a beat behind a second line, red mechanical indicator lamps, bellows folds drawn like an accordion-shaped waveform. Sparse vocal fragments limited to “one beat owed,” “relay late,” “change now,” and “debt cleared.”

## Track 3: Foldline Stops Before the Coast

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 150 BPM and begins with unusually long eight-bar PUSH passages. Maeve uses the same three-button shape throughout the first minute while Kervin gradually subtracts percussion instead of adding it. The first PULL arrives with no bass and almost no hats, exposing the sudden pitch change as the largest event in the arrangement. Solène answers with one dry electric-piano minor-seventh shell. Jamal uses low tanbou center hits only after reversals. In the final minute, PUSH and PULL alternate every half bar, then every beat, creating the fastest Fold Snap sequence on the EP while Kervin paradoxically simplifies the drums to kick and clap. The final reversal is left unresolved: Maeve begins PULL, releases pressure before the reeds fully stabilize, and the track ends on bellows breath and room tone before the expected bass response.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Cable route map descending toward the Caribbean coast but ending abruptly before the final station, folded concertina bellows drawn across the missing route, one red arrow turning back on itself. Sparse vocal fragments limited to “foldline stops,” “coast below,” “turn before,” and “no last response.”

